-- title:
--   Twisted centralizer of δ versus centralizer of its norm
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $L$ be number fields with $L/K$ finite Galois, and let $\sigma \in \mathrm{Gal}(L/K)$ be such that every $K$-automorphism of $L$ lies in the subgroup of integer powers of $\sigma$ (so the Galois group is cyclic, generated by $\sigma$); write $n = [L:K]$ and $\mathbb{A} = \mathbb{A}_K$ for the adele ring of $K$. Let $\gamma_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K)$ satisfy `IsRegularSemisimple`, i.e. $\operatorname{tr}(\gamma_0)^2 - 4\det(\gamma_0)$ is a unit of $K$; let $a \in K^\times$, let $u \in \mathbb{A}^\times$, let $\delta_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ and let $c \in (L \otimes_K \mathbb{A})^\times$. Assume: (i) $a\gamma_0$ is a norm representative of $\delta_0$, that is, there is $P \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ with $P^{-1}\,\delta_0\,\sigma(\delta_0)\cdots\sigma^{n-1}(\delta_0)\,P$ equal to the image of $a\gamma_0$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(L)$; (ii) the norm string $\prod_{i<n}\sigma^i$ of the scalar matrix $c$ equals the image $1 \otimes u$ of the scalar matrix $u$ under $\mathbb{A} \to L \otimes_K \mathbb{A}$. Put $\gamma = \gamma_0 \cdot (a u) \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A})$ (image of $\gamma_0$ times the scalar matrix with entry $a u$) and $\delta = (\delta_0 \otimes 1)\cdot c \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L \otimes_K \mathbb{A})$. Assume further that $y \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L \otimes_K \mathbb{A})$ is a norm conjugator for this pair, i.e. $1 \otimes \gamma = y^{-1}\,\big(\prod_{i<n}\sigma^i(\delta)\big)\,y$. Then, with all groups carrying their Borel structures, there is an isomorphism of topological groups $e$ from the $\sigma$-twisted centralizer $\{t : t\,\delta\,\sigma(t)^{-1} = \delta\}$ of $\delta$ onto the centralizer of $\{\gamma\}$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A})$ such that: (1) $1 \otimes e(t) = y^{-1} t y$ for all $t$; (2) $e(t)$ lies in the image under $\mathrm{GL}_2(K) \to \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A})$ of the centralizer of $\gamma_0$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(K)$ if and only if $t$ lies in the image under $\mathrm{GL}_2(L) \to \mathrm{GL}_2(L\otimes_K\mathbb{A})$ of $\{t_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L) : t_0\,\delta_0\,\sigma(t_0)^{-1} = \delta_0\}$; and (3) for every measure $\tau$ on the centralizer of $\gamma$ — no Haar or regularity condition is imposed — the pair $(\tau, (e^{-1})_*\tau)$ is coupled relative to $y$, meaning that the pushforward of $(e^{-1})_*\tau$ along $t \mapsto y^{-1} t y$ coincides with the pushforward of $\tau$ along $t \mapsto 1 \otimes t$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(L \otimes_K \mathbb{A})$.
--
--   This is the adelic form of the identification, for cyclic base change of $\mathrm{GL}_2$, of the $\sigma$-twisted centralizer of $\delta$ with the centralizer of its norm $\gamma$, together with the two supplements needed to compare elliptic and $\sigma$-elliptic terms: rational points correspond on both sides, and every measure on the centralizer transports to a coupled measure on the twisted centralizer. It is used in the comparison of covolumes and twisted orbital integrals for matching classes.
